.TH LN 1
.SH NAME
ln  \-  make a link
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ln
[
.B \-f
] name1 [ name2 ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A link is a directory entry referring
to a file; the same file (together with
its size, all its protection
information, etc.)
may have several links to it.
There is no way to distinguish a link to a file
from its original directory entry;
any changes in the
file are effective
independently of the name by which the file is known.
.PP
.I Ln
creates a link to an existing file
.IR name1 .
If
.I name2
is given, the link has that name;
otherwise it is placed in the current directory
and its name is the last component
of
.IR name1 .
If
.I name2
is a directory, the link is made inside it under the last component of
.IR name1 .
.PP
It is forbidden to link to a directory
or to link across file systems.
.PP
.B Note:
the SYNOPSIS above is corrected.  v7's page showed no options at all, and the
program has always taken
.BR \-f ,
which suppresses the refusal to link to a directory.  The refusal is
.IR ln 's
own; the kernel's is what stands behind it, and the two do not say the same
thing.
.IR Link (2)
here reads
.PP
.RS
.B "if ((ip->i_mode & IFMT) == IFDIR && !suser())"
.RE
.PP
\- kernel/sys2.c \- so linking to a directory is refused to an ordinary user and
.B permitted to the super\-user.
With
.BR \-f ,
therefore, root really can make a second name for a directory, and the result is
a filesystem no
.IR fsck
will thank you for: a cycle, or a directory with two parents and a `..' that can
only point at one of them.  Use
.IR mv (1),
which is set\-user\-id root precisely so that it can do this correctly.
.PP
`or to link across file systems' is the kernel's too \- it is
.B EXDEV
from the same routine \- and it cannot arise on this machine, one EC\-5052 drive
being the whole of the store.
.PP
.B Note:
when
.I name2
is a directory, the constructed name is refused if it will not fit 100
characters.  v7 built it with no bound at all.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Ln
exits 1 on any failure.  The first three messages go to the standard
.B output,
which is v7's and is inconsistent with its neighbours; only the last goes to the
standard error.
.PP
.B Usage: ln [ \-f ] target [ newname ]
.PP
.BI "ln: " name " does not exist"
.PP
.BI "ln: " name " is a directory"
\- and
.B \-f
was not given.
.PP
.BI "ln: " dir / name : " name too long"
.PP
.BI "ln: " reason
\- from
.IR perror (3),
when the
.IR link (2)
itself failed:
.B File exists
if the new name is taken,
.B Permission denied
if the directory it would go in is not writable.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
rm(1), cp(1), mv(1), link(2)
